From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: A process's owner can open their own processes and threads

Multi-user debt D18. A process object's default security descriptor
(process_get_sd) grants access to Administrators only, and threads had none at
all -- upstream never needed more, since every process there belongs to a user
who administers their own prefix. In a prefix shared between Unix users a
standard user could not open, suspend, read the context of, or set the
affinity of even their own processes: DebugActiveProcess, task managers and
crash handlers all failed with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED (measured: ntdll:info's
process suite, and a probe that OpenProcess/OpenThread/SuspendThread/
GetThreadContext a same-user process).

Windows gives a process's and thread's owner full access. In a shared prefix,
each new process and thread now gets a descriptor (sg_create_owner_sd) naming
the owning token's user, Local System and Administrators with full access, and
the user as owner -- exactly what a standard user gets for their own processes
on Windows. Ordinary prefixes are unchanged (the descriptor is set only with
the system-prefix marker).

Tested as a second Unix user on a shared server: a probe opens its own
process and thread (PROCESS_QUERY, THREAD_ALL_ACCESS), suspends the thread and
reads its context -- all granted; the same probe against a SYSTEM-owned
process is denied (ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED). SYSTEM still opens everything.
Conformance as the server's own user is unchanged.
